#!/usr/bin/env xetal
## LoRA explorer: one 8 x 8 projection adapted by a rank-r update. The
## frozen W, the trainable A (r x 8) and B (8 x r); the adapted layer
## y = W x + s B (A x) as a pipeline of equations, drawn as math, its
## factors kept apart; the update s B A, its rank and the merge; then
## what adapters cost on a large mixture-of-experts model.

"e:" u_se< "Equations"
"flow:" u_se< "Flow"
"lo:" u_se< "LoRA"

### Data

## The rank, alpha (the scale s is alpha / r), and the sizes for the
## costs: a SwiGLU expert (d 4096, f 14336), 64 experts a layer, 24 layers.
r := 3
alpha := 6.0
d := 4096
f := 14336
experts := 64
layers := 24
## W, A, B and the input x by a fixed ripple, the sine of a square (no
## chance). A real adapter starts with B at 0 and learns it; these are
## made up, to show the arrays.
i := f_loat r_ange 64
W := 8 8 r_eshape s_in 0.7 * i * i
j := f_loat r_ange r * 8
A := (r c_at 8) r_eshape 0.4 * c_os 1.1 * j * j
B := (8 c_at r) r_eshape 0.3 * s_in 0.9 * j * j
X := c_os 0.4 * 8 t_ake i
s := alpha lo:s_caling r
q := r - 1

### The adapted layer

## x goes two ways: through the frozen W, and through the adapter, A
## first (r numbers), then B (8); the two are added.
y := @ flow:p_ipe< c#"
   X $x
|> "h[k] | r" e:e_q< "sum j:=0..7 | A[k,j] * x[j]" $h
|> "u[i] | 8" e:e_q< "sum k:=0..q | B[i,k] * h[k]" $u
|> "y[i] | 8" e:e_q< "(sum j:=0..7 | W[i,j] * x[j]) + s * u[i]""#
## The same pipeline, drawn as math, a row a stage.
drawn := @ flow:m_ath< c#"
   X $x
|> "h[k] | r" e:e_q< "sum j:=0..7 | A[k,j] * x[j]" $h
|> "u[i] | 8" e:e_q< "sum k:=0..q | B[i,k] * h[k]" $u
|> "y[i] | 8" e:e_q< "(sum j:=0..7 | W[i,j] * x[j]) + s * u[i]""#
y

### The update and the merge

## The update s B A (8 x 8, of rank r at most), the merged matrix W + s
## B A, the update's rank, and how far the merged layer's output is from
## the factored one (under 1e-12: 1).
delta := lo:d_elta (A, B, s)
merged := lo:m_erge (W, A, B, s)
lo:r_ank delta
0.000000000001 > 'm_ax r_/ a_bs y - merged '+ '* i_nner X
## The numbers trained, against W's 64; the multiply-adds for one input,
## factored, and to make B A first.
(r lo:p_arams 8 8) c_at 64
r lo:c_osts 8 8

### On a large model

## One expert's adapters (gate, up, down), then their MiB in BF16: one
## expert, a layer of experts, every layer.
n := r lo:e_xpert d c_at f
mib := (16 lo:b_ytes n) / 1048576.0
n
mib c_at (mib * f_loat experts) c_at mib * f_loat experts * layers
